# Ubuntu analysis-pipeline configuration.
#
# Sourced, never executed. Every consumer resolves its own location first
# and exports REPO_ROOT before sourcing this file:
#
#     SCRIPT_DIR="$(cd -- "$(dirname -- "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")" && pwd)"
#     REPO_ROOT="$(cd -- "$SCRIPT_DIR/.." && pwd)"
#     . "$SCRIPT_DIR/pipeline-ubuntu.env"
#
# That is what makes the pipeline runnable from a checkout at any path
# rather than only from the /home/user/drakvuf-tools deployment the
# scripts were originally written against (issue #4). Nothing here may
# depend on $PWD, ~, or $HOME.
#
# Android and Windows get their own copy of this file rather than a
# shared one with per-OS branches: dirwatch hard-codes a single origin
# domain per invocation, so one launcher per OS is the design, and a
# per-OS config file is the smallest thing that matches it.

# Every value below is written as ${NAME:-default}, so anything already
# set in the environment wins. That is what lets a CI job point at a
# cached profile or a job-local state directory by exporting a variable,
# instead of patching this file or the scripts that read it
# (docs/workflow-ci.md, "Repository installation").

# --- Repository-owned assets (paths under the checkout) -----------------

# The sandbox TEMPLATE domain, not the golden `ubuntu`. Clones are made
# from this; the golden VM is never touched by an analysis run.
SANDBOX_DOMAIN="${SANDBOX_DOMAIN:-ubuntu_sandbox}"

DOMAIN_CONFIG="${DOMAIN_CONFIG:-$REPO_ROOT/misc/${SANDBOX_DOMAIN}.cfg}"

# LibVMI/DRAKVUF kernel profile. Passed to drakvuf/injector as -r, which
# is the only way it is ever resolved: clone domains are created with
# rewritten names and have no /etc/libvmi.conf stanza of their own.
# Untracked and host-generated -- see profiles/README.md to regenerate.
KERNEL_PROFILE="${KERNEL_PROFILE:-$REPO_ROOT/profiles/linux.json}"

# `xl restore` source, produced out-of-band once per sandbox template.
# clone-ubuntu.pl derives the same path independently, from FindBin and
# its own $origin argument, so the two cannot drift apart: the rule is
# "$REPO_ROOT/state/<origin domain>.bak" in both places.
#
# Overriding SAVED_STATE alone is not enough -- clone-ubuntu.pl does not
# read this file, so a CI job relocating the state must place the .bak at
# the derived path or symlink it there.
STATE_DIR="${STATE_DIR:-$REPO_ROOT/state}"
SAVED_STATE="${SAVED_STATE:-$STATE_DIR/${SANDBOX_DOMAIN}.bak}"

# --- Runtime data (fixed absolute paths, NOT repo-relative) -------------

# Per-OS subdirectories implement the OS-selection mechanism decided in
# issue #3: the submitter chooses the target OS by choosing where to
# submit, and one dirwatch instance watches each. The OS token is never
# parsed out of a filename or any other attacker-influenced byte.
# See docs/architecture/analysis-pipeline.md.
TARGET_OS="${TARGET_OS:-ubuntu}"
WATCH_FOLDER="${WATCH_FOLDER:-/malware_incoming/$TARGET_OS}"
SERVE_FOLDER="${SERVE_FOLDER:-/malware_processing/$TARGET_OS}"
OUTPUT_FOLDER="${OUTPUT_FOLDER:-/malware_finished/$TARGET_OS}"

# --- Guest-side staging -------------------------------------------------

# Where the sample is written inside the analysis guest. The filename is
# the sample's SHA-256, computed Dom0-side by both preconfig-ubuntu.sh
# and drakvuf-ubuntu.sh -- no component of this path is derived from the
# submitted filename, which is untrusted.
#
# This directory must already exist in the saved guest image; injector's
# writefile does not create parents. /home/carlos/Desktop is evidenced as
# present and writable by the six staged runs in
# docs/runbooks/verify-user-placeholder.md.
#
# GUEST_USER is still hard-coded here rather than discovered. That is
# unchanged from the previous behaviour and remains open M1 work.
GUEST_USER="${GUEST_USER:-carlos}"
GUEST_STAGING_DIR="${GUEST_STAGING_DIR:-/home/$GUEST_USER/Desktop}"

# --- Analysis-network DNS ----------------------------------------------

# Resolver written into the clone's /etc/resolv.conf.
#
# This used to be the clone's own gateway, 172.16.<vlan>.1. Nothing has
# ever listened on port 53 there: network-setup.sh sets ip_forward and a
# MASQUERADE rule but starts no resolver and installs no DNAT, so a query
# to that address reaches Dom0's INPUT chain and is answered with ICMP
# port-unreachable rather than forwarded. Name resolution in the guest
# therefore failed on every run to date -- unnoticed because the only
# evidenced runs fetched by IP and never resolved anything.
#
# 1.1.1.1 is consistent with the posture the lab already has rather than
# a new one: network-setup.sh:139 NATs every clone to the uplink, so
# clones already have unrestricted outbound IP access. Refusing only DNS
# was not containment, just a broken resolver.
#
# Resolution is still fully observed: tcpdump.sh captures the clone's
# whole xenbr1.<vlan> interface, and DNS is plaintext UDP/53, so every
# query and answer lands in the per-sample pcap regardless of which
# resolver serves it.
#
# One thing this does NOT do, stated plainly because it is easy to assume
# from a working resolver: it is not egress control. Clone containment is
# still unverified (KNOWN GAP, DRAFT-013) and the inter-clone FORWARD DROP
# rule may never install at all (KNOWN GAP, DRAFT-028).
#
# This value is forward-compatible with the planned simulated/
# controlled-internet modes and needs no follow-up work of its own.
# FakeNet-NG's MultiHost mode "simulate[s] the Internet by acting as the
# gateway for another machine" (docs/internals.md) and redirects by
# iptables REDIRECT in PREROUTING, so it catches traffic to ANY
# destination address -- its own documented example is traffic to
# 1.1.1.1. A clone's default route already points at the gateway, so once
# a responder runs there these queries are intercepted without touching
# this setting.
#
# Do not "fix" this back to $VLAN's gateway address. That is what it was,
# and it resolved nothing.
GUEST_NAMESERVER="${GUEST_NAMESERVER:-1.1.1.1}"

# --- Orchestration ------------------------------------------------------

# dirwatch watch mode: 1 = inotify poll, 0 = 1s sleep loop.
WATCH_MODE="${WATCH_MODE:-1}"

# A long-lived process inside the saved memory image that injector
# attaches to. It is a property of the .bak file, not of this config --
# re-saving the template almost certainly changes it.
INJECTION_PID="${INJECTION_PID:-1415}"

# Sizes dirwatch's worker pool AND the number of xenbr1.<n> VLAN
# sub-interfaces network-setup.sh must create. The two must agree: raise
# one without the other and a clone is handed a VLAN with no gateway.
#
# KNOWN GAP (DRAFT-036): the thread-slot index *is* the VLAN and the slot
# array is process-local, so a second per-OS watcher started today would
# allocate the same VLANs as this one. Running Android concurrently needs
# a host-global VLAN allocator first.
MAX_CLONES="${MAX_CLONES:-2}"

ANALYSIS_BRIDGE="${ANALYSIS_BRIDGE:-xenbr1}"